{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Your right hand, O HaShem, shatters the enemy</b>. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 15:6). According to the plain sense: Your right hand, which is adorned in strength — how great is its deed! — Your right hand, O HaShem, shatters the enemy. And how many verses follow this pattern: \"For behold, Your enemies, O HaShem, for behold, Your enemies shall perish\" (Psalms 92:10); \"How long shall the wicked, O HaShem, how long shall the wicked exult\" (Psalms 94:3); \"The floods have lifted up, O HaShem, the floods have lifted up their voice\" (Psalms 93:3). And many more like these. But this is not correct in my opinion, for the verses would be repeating words merely to say that it will always be so, without explaining the matter except in the second instance; and if it had said יְמִינְךָ ה' יְמִינְךָ ה' תִּרְעַץ אוֹיֵב, then it would be like those cited examples. And Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Exodus 15:6) that the meaning of <b>Your right hand, O HaShem</b> is that You are <b>adorned in strength; Your right hand, O HaShem, shatters the enemy</b>, which is like those cited examples. But it is more correct to say: <b>Your right hand, O HaShem</b> — it is adorned <b>in strength</b> to humble every proud and lofty one; <b>Your right hand, O HaShem, shatters the enemy</b> — with great strength. And he mentioned 'right hand' in both masculine and feminine forms, as in \"And behold, a hand was sent to me, and behold, in it was a scroll of a book\" (Ezekiel 2:9), and it is doubled in the manner of prophecies. And the way of truth — the enlightened will understand it from the first verses that I have explained. And thus they said (Exodus Rabbah 24:1): One of His hands was sinking them and one of His hands was saving them, for strength saves, as it is said: \"And now, please, let the strength of Adonai grow great\" (Numbers 14:17)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "Rashi's paraphrased gloss reads לְשׁוֹן רַשִׁ\"י, פְּשָׁטוֹ שֶׁל דָּבָר, yielding a run-on that I have separated with a dash for readability while preserving all content."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:08:29.035331238Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}